      "caveats": "The analysis is conceptual and notes that these prefigurative organizing processes are highly contextual and fragmented.",
      "what": "This paper conceptualizes child mobility activism—specifically mobility collectives, street experiments, and demonstrations—as ludic spaces that prefigure children's right to the mobile city. It analyzes how activists use these play-based spaces, which rely on real emotional stakes and disruptive rhythms, as a foundation for prefigurative organizing.",
      "why": "It connects the political theory of play with prefigurative politics, demonstrating how temporary, playful occupations of street space can serve as real-world organizing tools for children's urban mobility rights."
